WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.025 --> 00:00:06.003
[SOUND] So now we will discuss how to
penalize insertions and

00:00:06.003 --> 00:00:13.040
deletions in sequence alignment in a more
sophisticated way.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.020
Previously, we used naive scoring for
indels, where

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:19.290
we assigned fixed penalty sigma to each
indel.

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:22.130
However, this fixed penalty isn't good to

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:26.295
represent series of hundreds of
consecutive indels.

00:00:26.295 --> 00:00:32.780
And we will call this sequence of k
consecutive indels as single evolutionary

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:40.289
event gap rather than many evolutionary
events of small insertions and deletions.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.720
To model this situation in sequence
alignment algorithms, we'll

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:51.250
use a find gap penalty, where we use two
constants.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:58.480
One is sigma, which is a gap opening
penalty, and the second one is epsilon,

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:03.700
what, which is gap extension penalty.
And usually sigma is more than epsilon.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:10.200
Since starting a gap should be penalized
more than extending it, and the formula

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:16.600
to calculate penalty for a gap of length k
will be sigma plus epsilon times k

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:17.020
minus 1.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:21.540
So let's take a look now how we can use
this in our Manhattan grid.

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:29.830
Previously we had a single edges with
weight sigma to represent insertions and

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:35.380
deletions, and these edges were horizontal
and vertical edges in our Manhattan grid.

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:37.470
Now, we can add more edges.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:41.760
For example, we can add an edge of a
weight sigma

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:47.330
plus epsilon, which will represent a gap,
a single gap of length two.

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:53.000
And for our Manhattan grid we actually
need to add a lot of these edges.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:59.572
So we need to add a separate edge for all
gaps of length two.

00:01:59.572 --> 00:02:03.360
Edges for all gaps of lengths 3 and more
and

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.940
more, and we will need to add it vertical
and horizontally.

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:14.190
How many edges have we added to our graph?
Actually, we added edges proportionally

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:18.910
to n cube, where n is a length of
sequences which we are trying to compare.

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:23.240
Since sequence alignment algorithms' run
time is proportional to

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:27.320
the number of edges, our algorithm will be
quite slow.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:32.680
It will run proportionally to the n cube.
How we can improve

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.310
this running time of algorithm?

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:39.910
We can split our Manhattan grid into three
levels.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:42.960
Bottom level, middle level, and upper
level.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.162
Bottom level will represent insertions,
and will contain

00:02:47.162 --> 00:02:52.170
only edges, which goes vertically from
node to node.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:54.540
Middle level will represent matches and

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:58.350
mismatches, and will contain diagonal
edges.

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:03.560
Upper level will represent additions, and
will contain only horizontal edges

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:08.804
of our graph.
And still, we need to find a path

00:03:08.804 --> 00:03:14.200
between top left corner to the bottom
right corner.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:17.160
And we can move in our Manhattan grid, but

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:23.310
we also can move between these layers of
the grid.

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:27.910
So for example, we can take one edge
diagonally, and now if we

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:32.870
want to make insertion, we need to move to
the bottom

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:38.268
level, take one edge there, and move back
to the middle level.

00:03:38.268 --> 00:03:43.770
And these two edges will represent a
single gap,

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:48.480
where the sigma is a gap opening penalty
and epsilon

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:53.160
is a gap extension penalty.
After that, we can continue moving

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.970
on the middle level, for example, we found
a match between our sequences.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:05.990
And then, if you want to represent
deletion, we need to move to upper level,

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:13.060
make some movements there, and go back to
the middle level.

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:13.960
And we can finish

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:18.890
our path.
To model this mathematically,

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:23.640
we can have three different equations for
dynamic

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:28.760
programming for each level.
And lower level will use

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:34.360
information from lower level itself, and
the middle level, middle level

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:40.000
will use information from all three
levels, and upper

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:45.110
level will use information from upper
level itself and the middle level.

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:51.420
How many edges does this three level
Manhattan grid have?

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:55.410
Hint, is a degree of which node is quite
small.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:05:00.210
So each node in this three layered
Manhattan

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:03.980
will have only one, two, or three
neighbors.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:05.130
And the

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:10.292
number of edges is proportional to the n
square, so the

00:05:10.292 --> 00:05:13.990
run time of this algorithm will be the
same as before.

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:19.760
But currently we can use more
sophisticated affine gap

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:23.110
penalty than the native penalty which we
used before.

